WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.063
AWS IAM policies come in two flavors, and the difference is simple: what is the policy attached to?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.177
Here is a user.

00:00:09.187 --> 00:00:11.140
And here is an S3 bucket.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:15.380
An identity-based policy is attached to an IAM user, group, or role.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.500
It defines what that identity is allowed to do.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.708
The permission travels with the caller.

00:00:20.718 --> 00:00:24.004
So when the user asks for the bucket, the request is allowed.

00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:28.331
Now watch the same idea attach somewhere else.

00:00:28.341 --> 00:00:35.813
A resource-based policy attaches to the resource itself, like a bucket policy, and it names the principals who may come in.

00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:38.512
Same account?

00:00:38.522 --> 00:00:41.149
A single Allow from either policy is enough.

00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:47.741
But across accounts, the identity policy must allow the action, and the resource policy must allow the caller.

00:00:47.751 --> 00:00:50.677
If either side is missing, the request is denied.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.505
So next time you read a policy, ask first: what is this attached to?
